Java uses `if` / `else if` / `else` — the syntax is similar to C-family languages:

```java
int score = 85;

if (score >= 90) {
    System.out.println("A grade");
} else if (score >= 80) {
    System.out.println("B grade");
} else {
    System.out.println("Keep practicing");
}
```

Comparison operators: `==` (equal), `!=` (not equal), `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`.

Logical operators use **words** (unlike Python's `and`/`or`):

```java
int age = 20;
boolean hasId = true;

if (age >= 18 && hasId) {
    System.out.println("Allowed in");
}

if (age < 13 || age > 60) {
    System.out.println("Special discount");
}
```

- `&&` means AND — both must be true
- `||` means OR — either can be true
- `!` means NOT — flips a boolean

One-liner shortcut — the **ternary operator**:

```java
String result = (score >= 50) ? "Pass" : "Fail";
```

